var e={id:`halogen`,objectives:[`Name halogenoalkanes using IUPAC nomenclature; classify as 1°, 2° or 3°`,`Describe isomerism in halogenoalkanes including optical isomerism at chiral centres`,`Write equations and conditions for nucleophilic substitution: hydrolysis (NaOH(aq)), nitrile formation (KCN/alc), and primary amine formation (excess NH₃)`,`Outline the SN1 and SN2 mechanisms with curly arrows; explain when each is preferred`,`Describe the elimination of HBr from 2-bromopropane using hot concentrated alcoholic KOH`,`Compare reactivities of halogenoalkanes and chlorobenzene; explain in terms of C–Hal bond strength and π-overlap`,`Explain the inertness of fluoroalkanes and uses of CFCs as refrigerants/aerosols`,`Describe the mechanism by which CFCs destroy the ozone layer and the role of HFCs as replacements`],chapters:[{id:`hd1`,title:`Halogenoalkanes: Naming & Classification`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogenoalkanes (also called haloalkanes) are alkanes in which one or more hydrogen atoms have been replaced by a halogen (F, Cl, Br or I). They have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₁X. Their behaviour is dominated by the polar C–X bond, where carbon carries a partial positive charge (δ+) and the halogen carries a partial negative charge (δ−).`},{type:`h`,text:`Nomenclature (IUPAC rules)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Identify the longest carbon chain containing the halogen.`,`Number from the end nearest the halogen to give the lowest locant.`,`Name the halogen as a prefix: fluoro, chloro, bromo, iodo.`,`For multiple halogens: list alphabetically (bromo before chloro), use di-/tri-/tetra- for multiples of the same halogen.`,`Examples: CH₃CH₂Br = bromoethane; (CH₃)₂CHCl = 2-chloropropane; CHCl₃ = trichloromethane (chloroform); CCl₄ = tetrachloromethane.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Classification: 1°, 2° and 3°`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CLASSES OF HALOGENOALKANES`,blocks:[{type:`cards`,cards:[{title:`PRIMARY (1°)`,body:[`C bonded to ONE`,`other carbon`,`R–CH₂–X`,`CH₃CH₂Br`,`bromoethane`],color:`primary`},{title:`SECONDARY (2°)`,body:[`C bonded to TWO`,`other carbons`,`R₂CH–X`,`(CH₃)₂CHBr`,`2-bromopropane`],color:`accent`},{title:`TERTIARY (3°)`,body:[`C bonded to THREE`,`other carbons`,`R₃C–X`,`(CH₃)₃CBr`,`2-Br-2-Me-propane`],color:`pink`}]},{type:`note`,text:`classification controls SN1/SN2 mechanism preference`}]},caption:`Three classes of halogenoalkanes`},{type:`ul`,items:[`PRIMARY (1°): the carbon bearing the halogen is bonded to ONE other carbon (or none, e.g. CH₃X). General form R–CH₂–X.`,`SECONDARY (2°): the C–X carbon is bonded to TWO other carbons. General form R₂CH–X.`,`TERTIARY (3°): the C–X carbon is bonded to THREE other carbons. General form R₃C–X.`,`This classification matters because it controls which substitution mechanism (SN1 or SN2) is preferred.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Why polarity matters`,body:`The C–X bond is polar because halogens are more electronegative than carbon. This δ+ on carbon makes it a target for NUCLEOPHILES (electron-rich species like OH⁻, CN⁻, NH₃). Most reactions of halogenoalkanes are nucleophilic substitutions or eliminations driven by this polarity.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Halogenoalkanes have a polar C–X bond (δ+ on C).`,`Classified as 1°, 2°, 3° based on substitution at the C–X carbon.`,`Class controls the mechanism (SN1 vs SN2).`]}]},{id:`hd2`,title:`Isomerism in Halogenoalkanes`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogenoalkanes show several types of isomerism: chain isomerism, position isomerism, and (for chiral molecules) optical isomerism.`},{type:`h`,text:`Chain isomerism`},{type:`p`,text:`Different carbon skeletons. Example: C₄H₉Br can be 1-bromobutane (CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br) or 1-bromo-2-methylpropane ((CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br) — same molecular formula, different chain.`},{type:`h`,text:`Position isomerism`},{type:`p`,text:`Same chain, halogen in different positions. Example: C₃H₇Cl exists as 1-chloropropane (CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl) and 2-chloropropane ((CH₃)₂CHCl). They have very different reactivities — 2-chloropropane is secondary, more prone to SN1.`},{type:`h`,text:`Optical isomerism`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Occurs when the C bearing the halogen has 4 DIFFERENT groups attached (a chiral centre or stereocentre).`,`Example: 2-bromobutane, CH₃CHBrCH₂CH₃. The middle C has H, Br, CH₃ and C₂H₅ — four different groups.`,`Two non-superimposable mirror image forms exist (enantiomers).`,`Enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions (one (+), one (−)).`,`A 1:1 mixture is called a RACEMIC mixture and is optically inactive (rotations cancel).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`SN1 gives racemic products`,body:`Because SN1 goes through a planar carbocation intermediate, the nucleophile can attack from either face equally → produces a 50:50 mixture of both enantiomers (racemic). SN2, by contrast, gives INVERSION of configuration (Walden inversion) — only one stereoisomer is produced.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Chain, position and optical isomerism all possible.`,`Optical isomerism requires a chiral centre (4 different groups on C–X).`,`SN1 → racemic; SN2 → inversion of configuration.`]}]},{id:`hd3`,title:`Nucleophilic Substitution: Hydrolysis`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Hydrolysis is the reaction of a halogenoalkane with water (or aqueous OH⁻) to form an alcohol. It is the prototypical nucleophilic substitution reaction at A-level.`},{type:`h`,text:`The reaction`},{type:`ul`,items:[`CH₃CH₂Br + NaOH(aq) → CH₃CH₂OH + NaBr.`,`Conditions: AQUEOUS NaOH, REFLUX (gentle heating with condenser to prevent loss of volatile reactant).`,`Mechanism: nucleophilic substitution. OH⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon and displaces Br⁻.`,`The OH⁻ ion is a good nucleophile because it has a lone pair on the oxygen and a negative charge.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Hydrolysis without OH⁻`},{type:`p`,text:`Even pure water can hydrolyse halogenoalkanes, though more slowly. This is the basis of the silver nitrate test for halogenoalkanes:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Add ethanol (to dissolve the halogenoalkane) and aqueous AgNO₃.`,`Warm gently. The halogenoalkane hydrolyses: R–X + H₂O → R–OH + HX.`,`The X⁻ ions react with Ag⁺: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl (white), Ag⁺ + Br⁻ → AgBr (cream), Ag⁺ + I⁻ → AgI (yellow).`,`The TIME taken for the precipitate to appear measures the rate of hydrolysis.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Order of reactivity: I > Br > Cl > F`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`C–HAL BOND ENERGIES & REACTIVITY`,blocks:[{type:`bars`,yMax:500,items:[{label:`C–F`,value:484,valueLabel:`484 kJ`,color:`green`,sublabel:`unreactive`},{label:`C–Cl`,value:338,valueLabel:`338 kJ`,color:`accent`,sublabel:`moderate`},{label:`C–Br`,value:285,valueLabel:`285 kJ`,color:`pink`,sublabel:`reactive`},{label:`C–I`,value:213,valueLabel:`213 kJ`,color:`pink`,sublabel:`most reactive`}]},{type:`note`,text:`weaker bond → easier to break → faster substitution`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`C–Hal bond energies determine reactivity`},{type:`ul`,items:[`C–F is the strongest (484 kJ mol⁻¹) — fluoroalkanes are essentially unreactive.`,`C–Cl: 338 kJ mol⁻¹.`,`C–Br: 285 kJ mol⁻¹.`,`C–I: 213 kJ mol⁻¹ — the weakest, easiest to break.`,`Reactivity is therefore: iodoalkanes > bromoalkanes > chloroalkanes >> fluoroalkanes.`,`Bond strength matters more than bond polarity here — although C–F is the most polar, it is so strong that hydrolysis is impractical.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`R–Br + NaOH(aq) → R–OH + NaBr (reflux in water).`,`Reactivity order: R–I > R–Br > R–Cl >> R–F.`,`Determined by C–Hal bond strength (weaker = more reactive).`]}]},{id:`hd4`,title:`SN2 Mechanism (Bimolecular)`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`SN2 stands for Substitution Nucleophilic Bimolecular. The reaction occurs in a SINGLE STEP — the nucleophile attacks the carbon at the same time as the halide leaves.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`SN2 MECHANISM · ONE STEP`,blocks:[{type:`equation`,text:`Nu⁻  +  R–X  →  Nu–R  +  X⁻`,big:!0},{type:`panel`,title:`TRANSITION STATE`,body:[`[Nu...R...X]‡`,`partial bonds, 5-coordinate`],color:`primary`},{type:`note`,text:`rate = k[R-X][Nu⁻]  →  SECOND order`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`favoured by 1° halogenoalkanes (less steric hindrance)`},{type:`note`,text:`concerted: bond formation and bond breaking happen together`}]},caption:`SN2 — concerted single step via transition state`},{type:`h`,text:`Key features of SN2`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The nucleophile attacks from the OPPOSITE side to the halogen ("backside attack").`,`A 5-coordinate transition state forms briefly — partial Nu–C bond and partial C–X bond.`,`The molecule undergoes WALDEN INVERSION — like an umbrella turning inside out.`,`Rate equation: rate = k[R-X][Nu⁻]. The reaction is SECOND ORDER overall (first order in each reactant).`,`SN2 is favoured by PRIMARY halogenoalkanes (R–CH₂–X). Less steric hindrance allows the nucleophile to approach.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why SN2 disfavours tertiary`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Tertiary carbons (R₃C–X) are surrounded by three large alkyl groups.`,`These bulky groups physically BLOCK the nucleophile from attacking.`,`The transition state would be highly crowded — too high in energy.`,`Tertiary halogenoalkanes therefore prefer SN1 (no nucleophile attack on the crowded carbon).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`SN2 = "S two": single step, two species in RDS`,body:`In SN2, the rate-determining step (the only step) involves both the halogenoalkane AND the nucleophile. Doubling either concentration doubles the rate. This is the diagnostic feature.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`SN2: one step, backside attack, Walden inversion.`,`Rate = k[R-X][Nu⁻] — bimolecular.`,`Favoured by 1° halogenoalkanes (no steric hindrance).`]}]},{id:`hd5`,title:`SN1 Mechanism (Unimolecular)`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`SN1 stands for Substitution Nucleophilic Unimolecular. The reaction occurs in TWO STEPS, with formation of a CARBOCATION intermediate.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`SN1 MECHANISM · TWO STEPS`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`STEP 1 — SLOW (RDS)`,body:[`R₃C–X  →  R₃C⁺  +  X⁻`,`heterolytic cleavage; carbocation intermediate`],color:`pink`},{type:`arrow`,text:`↓`},{type:`panel`,title:`STEP 2 — FAST`,body:[`R₃C⁺  +  Nu⁻  →  Nu–CR₃`],color:`green`},{type:`note`,text:`rate = k[R-X]  →  FIRST order`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`only depends on the halogenoalkane (RDS)`},{type:`note`,text:`favoured by 3° halogenoalkanes (carbocation stabilised)`},{type:`note`,text:`3° carbocation stabilised by 3 alkyl groups (positive inductive effect)`}]},caption:`SN1 — two steps via carbocation intermediate`},{type:`h`,text:`The two steps`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Step 1 (slow, RDS): the C–X bond breaks heterolytically. Both electrons go to the halogen, leaving a CARBOCATION (R₃C⁺) and a halide ion. This is the rate-determining step.`,`Step 2 (fast): the nucleophile attacks the carbocation: R₃C⁺ + Nu⁻ → R₃C–Nu.`,`Rate equation: rate = k[R-X]. The reaction is FIRST ORDER overall — only the halogenoalkane appears in the rate law because the nucleophile is not in the RDS.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why SN1 favours tertiary`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Tertiary carbocations (R₃C⁺) are STABILISED by three alkyl groups donating electron density (positive inductive effect).`,`More substitution → more stabilisation → easier carbocation formation → faster SN1.`,`Stability order: 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl carbocation.`,`Primary carbocations are very unstable and almost never form — primary halogenoalkanes go via SN2 instead.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Stereochemistry consequence`},{type:`p`,text:`The carbocation is PLANAR (sp² hybridised). The nucleophile can attack from either face with equal probability. If the starting material had a chiral centre, the product is a 50:50 RACEMIC MIXTURE — the original stereochemistry is lost.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`SN1 vs SN2 summary`,body:`SN2: 1° halogenoalkanes, single step, Walden inversion, rate = k[R-X][Nu⁻]. SN1: 3° halogenoalkanes, two steps, racemisation, rate = k[R-X]. Secondary (2°) can go either way depending on conditions.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`SN1: two steps, carbocation intermediate, racemisation.`,`Rate = k[R-X] only — unimolecular RDS.`,`Favoured by 3° halogenoalkanes (carbocation stabilised by alkyl groups).`]}]},{id:`hd6`,title:`Nitriles & Amines from Halogenoalkanes`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogenoalkanes are versatile starting materials for synthesis. Two key transformations are conversion to NITRILES (using KCN) and to PRIMARY AMINES (using NH₃).`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`REACTIONS OF BROMOETHANE`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Reagent`,`Conditions`,`Product`,`Type`],colWidths:[70,90,70,60],rows:[[`NaOH(aq)`,`reflux, water`,`CH₃CH₂OH`,`subst (OH⁻)`],[`KCN(alc)`,`reflux, ethanol`,`CH₃CH₂CN`,`+1 C, nitrile`],[`NH₃(alc)`,`sealed, heat`,`CH₃CH₂NH₂`,`1° amine`],[`NaOH(alc)`,`reflux, hot`,`CH₂=CH₂`,`ELIMINATION`],[`Mg/dry ether`,`dry conditions`,`CH₃CH₂MgBr`,`Grignard`]]},{type:`note`,text:`aqueous NaOH → SUBSTITUTION (alcohol)`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`alcoholic NaOH → ELIMINATION (alkene)`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Key reactions of bromoethane`},{type:`h`,text:`Formation of nitriles`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Reagent: KCN (potassium cyanide) dissolved in ETHANOL (alcoholic conditions).`,`Mechanism: nucleophilic substitution. CN⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon, displacing X⁻.`,`Equation: CH₃CH₂Br + KCN → CH₃CH₂CN + KBr.`,`The product is propanenitrile (CH₃CH₂CN). NOTE: the carbon chain has GROWN BY ONE — this is a useful way to extend a carbon skeleton.`,`Conditions: REFLUX in ethanol. Use ethanol (not water) to prevent hydrolysis competing.`,`WARNING: KCN is extremely toxic.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Formation of primary amines`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Reagent: EXCESS NH₃ in ethanol, in a SEALED TUBE under pressure (because NH₃ is a gas).`,`Mechanism: nucleophilic substitution. The lone pair on N attacks the δ+ carbon.`,`Equation: CH₃CH₂Br + NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HBr (HBr immediately neutralised by excess NH₃ to form NH₄Br).`,`Excess NH₃ is essential — otherwise the primary amine reacts further to give secondary (R₂NH) and tertiary (R₃N) amines, and even quaternary ammonium salts (R₄N⁺X⁻).`,`The product is ethanamine (CH₃CH₂NH₂). The carbon chain length is unchanged.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Why excess NH₃?`,body:`The primary amine product (R-NH₂) is itself a nucleophile — even better than NH₃ because alkyl groups are electron-donating. So it can attack another R-X molecule, giving R₂NH (secondary). This continues to R₃N and R₄N⁺. Excess NH₃ ensures NH₃ is the most likely nucleophile to attack first.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`R-X + KCN (alc) → R-CN (nitrile) — chain extends by 1.`,`R-X + excess NH₃ (alc, sealed) → R-NH₂ (primary amine).`,`Both are nucleophilic substitutions.`]}]},{id:`hd7`,title:`Elimination: Forming Alkenes`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`When a halogenoalkane is heated with HOT, CONCENTRATED, ALCOHOLIC KOH (or NaOH), it undergoes ELIMINATION instead of substitution. The product is an alkene, formed by loss of HX (here, HBr) from adjacent carbons.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`ELIMINATION · LOSS OF HBr`,subtitle:`2-bromopropane + KOH (alcoholic, hot)`,blocks:[{type:`equation`,text:`CH₃–CHBr–CH₃  →  CH₃–CH=CH₂`,big:!0},{type:`panel`,title:`PRODUCT MIX`,body:[`propene + KBr + H₂O`],color:`primary`},{type:`compare`,left:{title:`ELIMINATION`,body:[`Hot CONC.`,`alcoholic KOH`,`→ alkene`],color:`pink`},right:{title:`SUBSTITUTION`,body:[`Warm DILUTE`,`aqueous KOH`,`→ alcohol`],color:`accent`}},{type:`note`,text:`OH⁻ acts as a BASE (removes H⁺) in elimination`},{type:`note`,text:`OH⁻ acts as a NUCLEOPHILE in substitution`}]},caption:`Elimination of HBr from 2-bromopropane`},{type:`h`,text:`The reaction`},{type:`ul`,items:[`CH₃CHBrCH₃ + KOH (alc, hot, conc) → CH₃CH=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O.`,`2-bromopropane → propene.`,`Conditions: alcoholic KOH (in ethanol), hot, concentrated.`,`OH⁻ acts as a BASE in this reaction (removes H⁺), not as a nucleophile.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Substitution vs elimination — same reagent, different conditions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`AQUEOUS NaOH, warm → SUBSTITUTION → alcohol (OH⁻ acts as nucleophile).`,`ALCOHOLIC NaOH, hot, concentrated → ELIMINATION → alkene (OH⁻ acts as base).`,`The same reagent (OH⁻) gives different products depending on the solvent and conditions.`,`In aqueous solution, OH⁻ is highly solvated → less nucleophilic and behaves more as a base.`]},{type:`h`,text:`The mechanism (E2)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`OH⁻ removes a proton (H⁺) from a carbon ADJACENT to the C–Br carbon.`,`Simultaneously, the C–Br bond breaks, with both electrons going to Br⁻.`,`The two electrons released by the C–H bond form the new C=C double bond.`,`This is a single-step concerted mechanism (similar concept to SN2).`]},{type:`h`,text:`When more than one alkene is possible`},{type:`p`,text:`For 2-bromobutane, elimination can produce either but-1-ene (CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃) or but-2-ene (CH₃CH=CHCH₃). The more substituted alkene (but-2-ene) is generally the major product (Saytzeff's rule), because it is more thermodynamically stable.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Hot conc. alcoholic KOH → ELIMINATION → alkene.`,`Aqueous KOH → SUBSTITUTION → alcohol.`,`OH⁻ as BASE (elim) vs NUCLEOPHILE (sub).`,`Saytzeff: more substituted alkene is the major product.`]}]},{id:`hd8`,title:`Halogenoalkanes vs Chlorobenzene`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Chloroalkanes (e.g. chloroethane) hydrolyse readily. Chlorobenzene (where Cl is attached to a benzene ring) does NOT hydrolyse under normal conditions. This is one of the most asked exam comparisons.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CHLOROBENZENE vs CHLOROALKANE`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`CHLOROBENZENE`,body:[`C₆H₅–Cl`,`does NOT hydrolyse`,`C–Cl shorter & stronger`],color:`primary`},right:{title:`CHLOROETHANE`,body:[`CH₃CH₂Cl`,`readily hydrolyses`,`standard SN reactivity`],color:`accent`}},{type:`panel`,title:`WHY?`,body:[`Cl lone pair overlaps with π system`,`→ partial double bond character`,`C–Cl bond shorter and stronger`],color:`primary`},{type:`note`,text:`delocalisation makes the Cl very difficult to displace`}]},caption:`Why chlorobenzene resists hydrolysis`},{type:`h`,text:`Reactivity comparison`},{type:`ul`,items:[`CH₃CH₂Cl + NaOH(aq) → CH₃CH₂OH + NaCl (works under reflux).`,`C₆H₅Cl + NaOH(aq) → NO REACTION under normal conditions.`,`Chlorobenzene only hydrolyses under EXTREME conditions: 300 °C and 200 atm with concentrated NaOH.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why is chlorobenzene unreactive?`},{type:`ul`,items:[`In chlorobenzene, the Cl atom's lone pair OVERLAPS with the π electron system of the benzene ring.`,`This creates partial double-bond character in the C–Cl bond — the bond is shorter and stronger than in chloroalkanes.`,`The C–Cl bond energy in chlorobenzene (~399 kJ mol⁻¹) is significantly higher than in chloroethane (~338 kJ mol⁻¹).`,`The delocalisation of the chlorine lone pair into the ring makes it harder to displace Cl⁻.`,`Additionally, the benzene ring is electron-rich (π electrons) — it REPELS nucleophiles, making attack at the C–Cl carbon difficult.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`The resonance argument`,body:`Draw the lone pair on Cl overlapping with the ring π system. The C–Cl bond gains partial double-bond character. This stabilisation is lost if Cl is displaced, so the activation energy for substitution is much higher than in chloroalkanes. State this clearly in exams.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Chlorobenzene does NOT hydrolyse under normal conditions.`,`Cl lone pair overlaps with benzene π → partial double-bond character.`,`C–Cl bond is stronger and shorter → harder to break.`,`Electron-rich ring also repels nucleophiles.`]}]},{id:`hd9`,title:`Fluoroalkanes & CFCs`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Fluoroalkanes (and chlorofluorocarbons, CFCs) have unique properties due to the very strong, very inert C–F bond. This makes them useful for many applications, but also caused a major environmental crisis.`},{type:`h`,text:`Why fluoroalkanes are unreactive`},{type:`ul`,items:[`C–F bond energy: 484 kJ mol⁻¹ (compared to C–H = 412, C–Cl = 338).`,`C–F is the strongest single bond in organic chemistry.`,`F is the most electronegative element → bond is highly polar but also extremely strong.`,`Hydrolysis, oxidation and most other reactions are impractically slow at normal temperatures.`,`Examples of inertness: PTFE (Teflon, (CF₂CF₂)ₙ) is used as a non-stick coating because nothing sticks to it or reacts with it.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Uses of fluoroalkanes and CFCs`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Refrigerants (CFCs like CFCl₃, CF₂Cl₂): low boiling points, non-flammable, non-toxic to humans.`,`Aerosol propellants: easily liquefiable, chemically inert.`,`Fire extinguishers (e.g. halons like CF₃Br).`,`Solvents and degreasing agents.`,`PTFE (Teflon): non-stick cookware, gaskets, lab tubing.`,`HFCs (no Cl, e.g. CH₂F-CF₃): modern refrigerant replacements for CFCs.`]},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CFCs & THE OZONE LAYER`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`1. UV BREAKS C–Cl BOND (homolytic)`,body:[`CCl₃F → •CCl₂F + Cl•`],color:`pink`},{type:`panel`,title:`2. Cl RADICAL ATTACKS OZONE`,body:[`Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`3. ClO REGENERATES Cl (chain)`,body:[`ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂`],color:`primary`},{type:`note`,text:`One Cl• destroys ~100,000 O₃`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`chlorine is a CATALYST — regenerated each cycle`},{type:`panel`,title:`SOLUTION`,body:[`Replace CFCs with HFCs (no Cl)`,`or HCFCs (less stable)`],hint:`Montreal Protocol 1987`,color:`green`}]},caption:`CFCs catalytically destroy ozone`},{type:`h`,text:`CFCs and ozone depletion`},{type:`ul`,items:[`CFCs are so stable they reach the stratosphere over decades without breaking down.`,`High-energy UV radiation in the stratosphere breaks the relatively weaker C–Cl bond (homolytic fission): CFCl₃ → •CFCl₂ + Cl•.`,`The chlorine radical destroys ozone in a catalytic cycle: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂.`,`One Cl• can destroy thousands of O₃ molecules before being removed.`,`Ozone depletion → "ozone hole" over Antarctica → more UV reaches Earth's surface → increased skin cancer, cataracts, ecosystem damage.`]},{type:`h`,text:`The Montreal Protocol (1987)`},{type:`p`,text:`International agreement to phase out CFCs. Replacements include HFCs (hydrofluorocarbons — no chlorine, so no ozone damage) and HCFCs (hydrochlorofluorocarbons — less stable, mostly destroyed before reaching stratosphere). The ozone layer is now slowly recovering.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Fluoroalkanes: very inert (strong C–F bond) → useful in refrigerants, PTFE, propellants.`,`CFCs destroy ozone via Cl• catalytic cycle (1 Cl• → 1000s of O₃ destroyed).`,`Montreal Protocol replaced CFCs with HFCs (no Cl).`]}]},{id:`hd10`,title:`Synthesis Pathways via Halogenoalkanes`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogenoalkanes are central to organic synthesis because they can be converted into so many functional groups. Here is a summary "synthesis map".`},{type:`h`,text:`From halogenoalkane (R-X) you can make:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Alcohol: R-X + NaOH(aq), reflux → R-OH.`,`Alkene: R-X + KOH(alc, hot, conc) → alkene + HX.`,`Nitrile: R-X + KCN(alc), reflux → R-CN. Adds one carbon.`,`Primary amine: R-X + excess NH₃(alc, sealed) → R-NH₂.`,`Ether: R-X + R'O⁻Na⁺ → R-O-R' (Williamson synthesis — beyond syllabus but useful).`,`Grignard reagent: R-X + Mg(dry ether) → R-MgX. Used in C-C bond formation (advanced).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Going further from these intermediates`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Nitrile (R-CN) can be HYDROLYSED to a carboxylic acid: R-CN + 2H₂O + HCl → R-COOH + NH₄Cl. This is the standard way to make a carboxylic acid with one more carbon than the starting material.`,`Nitrile (R-CN) can be REDUCED with H₂/Ni or LiAlH₄ to give a primary amine: R-CN → R-CH₂NH₂.`,`Alcohol can be oxidised to aldehyde or carboxylic acid (1°), or ketone (2°).`,`Alkene undergoes addition reactions (covered in hydrocarbons).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Building longer chains`,body:`The KCN reaction is a key chain-extending step. R-X (n carbons) + CN⁻ → R-CN (n+1 carbons) → hydrolysis to R-COOH (n+1 carbons), or reduction to R-CH₂NH₂ (n+1 carbons). This is the only common way at A-level to make a chain longer.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`R-X is a synthetic hub: → alcohols, alkenes, nitriles, amines.`,`KCN extends chain by 1: R-X → R-CN → R-COOH or R-CH₂NH₂.`,`Conditions select between substitution (alcohol) and elimination (alkene).`]}]},{id:`hd11`,title:`Exam Technique & Common Pitfalls`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogenoalkane questions test mechanism, conditions, and product prediction. Here are the patterns.`},{type:`h`,text:`Mechanism questions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`When asked to "show the mechanism", use CURLY ARROWS correctly. Each arrow shows the movement of an electron PAIR, from where it is to where it goes.`,`Show lone pairs on the nucleophile (e.g. on OH⁻ or NH₃) before they attack.`,`Show partial charges (δ+ on C, δ− on X) on the C–X bond.`,`For SN2: ONE concerted arrow (from Nu to C, simultaneously another from C–X bond to X).`,`For SN1: TWO steps. Step 1: arrow from C–X bond to X (leaving). Step 2: arrow from Nu lone pair to C+.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Conditions matter — read the question`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Aqueous NaOH/KOH → SUBSTITUTION → alcohol.`,`Alcoholic NaOH/KOH (hot, conc) → ELIMINATION → alkene.`,`Alcoholic KCN → SUBSTITUTION → nitrile (chain +1).`,`NH₃ in ethanol, sealed tube → SUBSTITUTION → primary amine.`,`Stating the wrong conditions costs marks even if the equation is correct.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Common mistakes`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Forgetting that NH₃ must be in EXCESS (otherwise multiple substitutions occur).`,`Saying CN⁻ "attacks the H" (wrong — it attacks the C).`,`Using "nucleophile" and "base" interchangeably. OH⁻ can be both, but in different contexts.`,`Drawing chlorobenzene undergoing hydrolysis (it doesn't under normal conditions).`,`Confusing Walden inversion (SN2) with racemisation (SN1).`,`Not stating that KCN is in ALCOHOLIC solution (water would compete via hydrolysis).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`The classification → mechanism rule`,body:`Primary halogenoalkane → SN2 (no steric issue, no carbocation stability). Tertiary → SN1 (carbocation stabilised, but too crowded for SN2). Secondary → can go either way depending on conditions. State this in exam answers.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Aqueous → substitution; alcoholic + hot + conc → elimination.`,`1° → SN2; 3° → SN1; 2° → either.`,`Use curly arrows correctly; show lone pairs and partial charges.`,`NH₃ must be in excess for clean primary amine.`]}]}],quizzes:[{q:`The IUPAC name for (CH₃)₂CHBr is:`,opts:[`1-bromopropane`,`2-bromopropane`,`isopropyl bromide`,`1-methyl-1-bromoethane`],ans:1,hint:`Br is on the second carbon of propane.`},{q:`A primary halogenoalkane has the carbon bearing the halogen bonded to:`,opts:[`Zero or one other carbon`,`Two other carbons`,`Three other carbons`,`Four other carbons`],ans:0,hint:`Primary = one or zero alkyl groups on the C–X carbon.`},{q:`(CH₃)₃CBr is classified as:`,opts:[`Primary`,`Secondary`,`Tertiary`,`Quaternary`],ans:2,hint:`C–Br carbon has 3 methyl groups attached.`},{q:`Which has the strongest C–X bond?`,opts:[`C–F`,`C–Cl`,`C–Br`,`C–I`],ans:0,hint:`C–F is 484 kJ mol⁻¹ — the strongest.`},{q:`Which halogenoalkane reacts FASTEST with NaOH(aq)?`,opts:[`Fluoroethane`,`Chloroethane`,`Bromoethane`,`Iodoethane`],ans:3,hint:`C–I is the weakest bond → easiest to break.`},{q:`The reaction CH₃CH₂Br + NaOH(aq) → CH₃CH₂OH + NaBr is classified as:`,opts:[`Electrophilic addition`,`Nucleophilic substitution`,`Electrophilic substitution`,`Free radical`],ans:1,hint:`OH⁻ (nucleophile) replaces Br⁻ on the carbon.`},{q:`In SN2, the rate depends on:`,opts:[`[R-X] only`,`[Nu⁻] only`,`[R-X] and [Nu⁻]`,`Neither`],ans:2,hint:`Bimolecular: both reactants in the rate-determining (only) step.`},{q:`The transition state in SN2 has the carbon:`,opts:[`Bonded to 3 groups`,`Bonded to 4 groups`,`Bonded to 5 groups (partial)`,`Free as a carbocation`],ans:2,hint:`Pentacoordinate transition state — partial bonds to Nu and X.`},{q:`SN2 produces:`,opts:[`Racemic mixture`,`Walden inversion (one isomer with inverted configuration)`,`Both enantiomers equally`,`No stereochemical change`],ans:1,hint:`Backside attack inverts the configuration like an umbrella.`},{q:`In SN1, the rate equation is:`,opts:[`rate = k[R-X]`,`rate = k[Nu]`,`rate = k[R-X][Nu]`,`rate = k[R-X]²`],ans:0,hint:`Only R-X appears because it is the only species in the RDS.`},{q:`SN1 forms a:`,opts:[`Carbanion intermediate`,`Carbocation intermediate`,`Free radical`,`5-coordinate transition state`],ans:1,hint:`C–X breaks heterolytically; both electrons go to X.`},{q:`Tertiary halogenoalkanes prefer SN1 because:`,opts:[`Carbocation is stabilised by alkyl groups`,`They are smaller`,`They have weaker bonds`,`They are polar`],ans:0,hint:`Three alkyl groups donate electrons → stabilise R₃C⁺.`},{q:`Primary halogenoalkanes prefer SN2 because:`,opts:[`Carbocation would be very stable`,`No steric hindrance for nucleophile attack`,`They are more polar`,`They have weaker bonds`],ans:1,hint:`Less crowding around the C means the nucleophile can approach.`},{q:`SN1 produces a racemic mixture because:`,opts:[`Both enantiomers form simultaneously`,`The carbocation is planar — Nu attacks from either face`,`The mechanism is single-step`,`Walden inversion always occurs`],ans:1,hint:`Planar C⁺ allows attack from above or below equally.`},{q:`CH₃CH₂Br + KCN(alcoholic) → ?`,opts:[`CH₃CH₂OH`,`CH₃CH₂CN`,`CH₃CN`,`CH₂=CH₂`],ans:1,hint:`CN⁻ replaces Br⁻ — nitrile formed with one extra carbon.`},{q:`Why is KCN dissolved in ethanol, not water?`,opts:[`CN⁻ is more reactive in ethanol`,`Water would competitively hydrolyse the halogenoalkane`,`Ethanol is cheaper`,`KCN is insoluble in water`],ans:1,hint:`Water + halogenoalkane → alcohol (competing reaction).`},{q:`CH₃CH₂Br + excess NH₃ in ethanol →`,opts:[`CH₃CH₂OH`,`CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HBr`,`CH₃CN`,`CH₃CH₂N(CH₂CH₃)₂`],ans:1,hint:`NH₃ acts as nucleophile; lone pair attacks C.`},{q:`Why must NH₃ be in EXCESS in this reaction?`,opts:[`To dissolve the halogenoalkane`,`To prevent multiple substitutions giving secondary/tertiary amines`,`NH₃ is unreactive`,`To act as a catalyst`],ans:1,hint:`Without excess, the primary amine would react further.`},{q:`Hot concentrated alcoholic KOH with 2-bromopropane gives:`,opts:[`Propan-2-ol`,`Propene`,`Propanal`,`Propanenitrile`],ans:1,hint:`Elimination conditions → alkene.`},{q:`In elimination, OH⁻ acts as a:`,opts:[`Nucleophile (attacks C)`,`Base (removes H)`,`Catalyst`,`Acid`],ans:1,hint:`It deprotonates a β-carbon, not attacking the α-carbon.`},{q:`Aqueous NaOH gives substitution; alcoholic NaOH gives elimination because:`,opts:[`Different concentrations`,`In water, OH⁻ is solvated and acts as nucleophile; in ethanol, less solvated and acts as base`,`Water is a catalyst`,`Ethanol participates in the reaction`],ans:1,hint:`Solvation affects whether OH⁻ acts as Nu or base.`},{q:`Chlorobenzene does NOT hydrolyse readily because:`,opts:[`Cl is too small`,`C–Cl bond has partial double-bond character due to overlap with ring π electrons`,`Benzene is unreactive`,`Cl⁻ is a poor leaving group`],ans:1,hint:`Lone pair on Cl delocalises into the ring → stronger C–Cl.`},{q:`CFCs damage the ozone layer by releasing:`,opts:[`Cl⁻ ions`,`Cl radicals`,`F radicals`,`CO₂`],ans:1,hint:`UV breaks C–Cl homolytically → Cl• which catalytically destroys O₃.`},{q:`Cl• + O₃ →`,opts:[`ClO⁻ + O₂`,`ClO• + O₂`,`Cl⁻ + 3O`,`No reaction`],ans:1,hint:`Radical mechanism: Cl takes one O.`},{q:`In CFCs damaging the ozone, Cl is acting as a:`,opts:[`Reactant consumed`,`Catalyst (regenerated each cycle)`,`Solvent`,`Spectator`],ans:1,hint:`ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂ — Cl returns.`},{q:`PTFE (Teflon) is unreactive because:`,opts:[`It is ionic`,`C–F bonds are very strong (484 kJ mol⁻¹)`,`It is shielded by polymer chains`,`It is hydrophobic`],ans:1,hint:`Strong C–F bonds → essentially inert.`},{q:`The reaction of bromoethane with KCN extends the carbon chain by:`,opts:[`0 carbons`,`1 carbon`,`2 carbons`,`3 carbons`],ans:1,hint:`CN⁻ adds the cyanide carbon: C₂ → C₃.`},{q:`Hydrolysis of nitrile R-CN gives:`,opts:[`R-OH`,`R-COOH`,`R-NH₂`,`R-CHO`],ans:1,hint:`Acid hydrolysis: R-CN + 2H₂O → R-COOH + NH₃.`},{q:`In the AgNO₃ test, iodoethane gives:`,opts:[`White ppt fastest`,`Cream ppt fastest`,`Yellow ppt fastest`,`No ppt`],ans:2,hint:`AgI is yellow; iodide reacts fastest because C-I is weakest.`},{q:`A halogenoalkane that gives a racemic product on hydrolysis is most likely:`,opts:[`Primary`,`Secondary`,`Tertiary`,`Achiral`],ans:2,hint:`Tertiary goes via SN1 → planar carbocation → attack from either face.`},{q:`CH₃CHBrCH₂CH₃ has a chiral centre because:`,opts:[`It has a Br atom`,`The C–Br carbon has 4 different groups (H, Br, CH₃, C₂H₅)`,`It is asymmetric`,`It is a 2° halogenoalkane`],ans:1,hint:`4 different groups around C = chiral centre.`},{q:`The C–F bond is the strongest single bond in organic chemistry. The bond energy is approximately:`,opts:[`338 kJ mol⁻¹`,`412 kJ mol⁻¹`,`484 kJ mol⁻¹`,`944 kJ mol⁻¹`],ans:2,hint:`C–F = 484. (944 is N≡N triple bond.)`}],multi:[],structured:[{title:`SN2 mechanism`,marks:8,q:`1-bromopropane is reacted with aqueous sodium hydroxide.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the reaction.`,marks:1,ans:`CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + NaOH → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + NaBr.`},{q:`(b) Classify 1-bromopropane (1°/2°/3°) and predict whether it will react via SN1 or SN2.`,marks:2,ans:`1-bromopropane is PRIMARY (the C–Br carbon has only ONE other carbon attached). It will react via SN2 — primary halogenoalkanes prefer SN2 because there is little steric hindrance and a primary carbocation would be too unstable for SN1.`},{q:`(c) Outline the mechanism using curly arrows. Identify the nucleophile and describe the transition state.`,marks:4,ans:`OH⁻ (nucleophile, with lone pair) attacks the C bearing Br from the OPPOSITE side. As the new C–O bond forms, the C–Br bond breaks, with both electrons going to Br. Two curly arrows: one from OH⁻ to C, one from C–Br bond to Br. Transition state: 5-coordinate carbon with partial Nu–C bond and partial C–X bond. Walden inversion occurs (configuration flips).`},{q:`(d) State the rate equation and the overall order.`,marks:1,ans:`rate = k[CH₃CH₂CH₂Br][OH⁻]. Overall order = 2 (bimolecular).`}]},{title:`SN1 vs SN2 comparison`,marks:9,q:`Compare the hydrolysis of 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (a 3° halogenoalkane) with 1-bromobutane (a 1° halogenoalkane).`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the hydrolysis of 2-bromo-2-methylpropane.`,marks:1,ans:`(CH₃)₃CBr + NaOH → (CH₃)₃COH + NaBr (or with H₂O: (CH₃)₃CBr + H₂O → (CH₃)₃COH + HBr).`},{q:`(b) Predict the mechanism for each compound and justify.`,marks:2,ans:`2-bromo-2-methylpropane (3°): SN1. The carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺ is stabilised by three alkyl groups donating electron density. The bulky tertiary carbon also disfavours SN2. 1-bromobutane (1°): SN2. No steric hindrance for nucleophile attack; primary carbocation would be too unstable for SN1.`},{q:`(c) Write the mechanism for the SN1 reaction in step-by-step detail.`,marks:4,ans:`Step 1 (slow, RDS): heterolytic cleavage of C–Br bond. (CH₃)₃CBr → (CH₃)₃C⁺ + Br⁻. Curly arrow from C–Br bond to Br. Step 2 (fast): nucleophile attacks the carbocation. (CH₃)₃C⁺ + OH⁻ → (CH₃)₃COH. Curly arrow from OH⁻ lone pair to C⁺.`},{q:`(d) State and explain the rate equations for each mechanism.`,marks:2,ans:`SN1: rate = k[(CH₃)₃CBr]. First order — only the halogenoalkane appears because the slow step (Step 1) does not involve the nucleophile. SN2: rate = k[CH₃(CH₂)₃Br][OH⁻]. Second order — both reactants are in the single (rate-determining) step.`}]},{title:`Substitution vs elimination`,marks:8,q:`2-bromopropane can react with potassium hydroxide under different conditions.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for 2-bromopropane + AQUEOUS KOH.`,marks:1,ans:`(CH₃)₂CHBr + KOH(aq) → (CH₃)₂CHOH + KBr (propan-2-ol).`},{q:`(b) Write the equation for 2-bromopropane + HOT CONCENTRATED ALCOHOLIC KOH.`,marks:1,ans:`(CH₃)₂CHBr + KOH(alc) → CH₃CH=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O (propene).`},{q:`(c) Identify the type of reaction in each case.`,marks:2,ans:`Aqueous: nucleophilic substitution. OH⁻ acts as a nucleophile, replacing Br. Alcoholic, hot, concentrated: elimination. OH⁻ acts as a base, removing H from the adjacent carbon, with simultaneous loss of Br⁻.`},{q:`(d) Explain why the SAME reagent (KOH) gives different products depending on conditions.`,marks:4,ans:`In aqueous solution, OH⁻ is heavily solvated by hydrogen bonding to water molecules. This makes it less effective as a base but still reactive enough to act as a nucleophile, attacking the δ+ carbon. In ethanol (less polar, less hydrogen bonding), OH⁻ is less solvated and more reactive as a BASE — it preferentially removes a proton from the adjacent C–H. Heat and concentration also favour elimination (entropy: more particles produced; energy: enough to overcome higher Ea of E2). The competing factors mean conditions determine the major product.`}]},{title:`Halogenoalkane synthesis`,marks:8,q:`Bromoethane is a versatile starting material in organic synthesis.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation and conditions to convert bromoethane to ethanol.`,marks:1,ans:`CH₃CH₂Br + NaOH(aq) → CH₃CH₂OH + NaBr. Conditions: reflux with aqueous NaOH.`},{q:`(b) Write the equation and conditions to convert bromoethane to propanenitrile.`,marks:2,ans:`CH₃CH₂Br + KCN(alc) → CH₃CH₂CN + KBr. Conditions: reflux with KCN dissolved in ethanol. Note: the carbon chain extends by one carbon (C₂ → C₃).`},{q:`(c) Write the equation and conditions to convert bromoethane to ethanamine.`,marks:2,ans:`CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br. Conditions: heat with EXCESS ammonia in ethanol, in a SEALED TUBE under pressure (NH₃ is a gas).`},{q:`(d) Explain why excess ammonia is essential in part (c).`,marks:3,ans:`Without excess NH₃, the primary amine product (CH₃CH₂NH₂) is itself a nucleophile — even more reactive than NH₃ because alkyl groups are electron-donating. It can attack another bromoethane molecule, giving a SECONDARY amine ((CH₃CH₂)₂NH). This continues to give tertiary amine ((CH₃CH₂)₃N) and even quaternary ammonium salt ((CH₃CH₂)₄N⁺Br⁻). Excess NH₃ ensures NH₃ is the most likely nucleophile to attack first, giving mainly the desired primary amine.`}]},{title:`Chlorobenzene unreactivity`,marks:7,q:`Chloroethane reacts readily with aqueous NaOH, but chlorobenzene does NOT under the same conditions.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the reaction of chloroethane with aqueous NaOH.`,marks:1,ans:`CH₃CH₂Cl + NaOH(aq) → CH₃CH₂OH + NaCl.`},{q:`(b) Explain why chlorobenzene does not hydrolyse under normal conditions.`,marks:4,ans:`In chlorobenzene, the chlorine's lone pair OVERLAPS with the π electron system of the benzene ring (delocalisation). This creates partial double-bond character in the C–Cl bond, making it shorter and stronger than in chloroethane (~399 vs ~338 kJ mol⁻¹). Additionally, the electron-rich benzene ring REPELS the incoming nucleophile (OH⁻), making attack at the C–Cl carbon energetically unfavourable. Together, these effects raise the activation energy so high that hydrolysis does not occur under normal conditions.`},{q:`(c) State the conditions under which chlorobenzene CAN be hydrolysed.`,marks:2,ans:`Very harsh conditions are required: temperatures around 300 °C and pressures around 200 atm, with concentrated NaOH. Industrially this was used in the Dow process to make phenol from chlorobenzene. The high temperature provides enough energy to overcome the high activation energy.`}]},{title:`CFCs and the ozone layer`,marks:7,q:`Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) like CFCl₃ have caused damage to the ozone layer.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain why CFCs were widely used as refrigerants.`,marks:2,ans:`CFCs have low boiling points (easily liquefied and evaporated for refrigeration cycles). The C–F bonds are very strong (484 kJ mol⁻¹), making CFCs chemically INERT, non-flammable and non-toxic to humans. They are also stable, odourless, and have low cost.`},{q:`(b) Write equations to show how CFCs destroy ozone in the stratosphere.`,marks:3,ans:`Initiation: UV radiation breaks the C–Cl bond homolytically: CFCl₃ → •CFCl₂ + Cl•. Propagation step 1: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂. Propagation step 2: ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂ (Cl• is regenerated). The Cl atom acts as a CATALYST — it can destroy thousands of ozone molecules before being removed.`},{q:`(c) Explain why HFCs (e.g. CH₂FCF₃) are now used as replacements.`,marks:2,ans:`HFCs contain no chlorine atoms. When UV breaks any bonds, no Cl radicals are produced, so they cannot catalyse the destruction of ozone. They retain the useful properties of CFCs (low b.p., non-flammable, non-toxic) without the environmental damage. Some HFCs are still greenhouse gases, however.`}]}],worked:[{title:`Predict mechanism`,diff:`easy`,q:`For (CH₃)₃CCl reacting with KOH, predict the mechanism and product.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`(CH₃)₃CCl is TERTIARY (the C–Cl carbon has 3 methyl groups).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Tertiary halogenoalkanes prefer SN1 because the carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺ is stabilised by three alkyl groups (positive inductive effect).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`The reaction is a substitution: Cl⁻ leaves, OH⁻ attacks the carbocation.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Product: (CH₃)₃COH (2-methylpropan-2-ol). Mechanism: SN1.`}],ans:`(CH₃)₃COH via SN1 mechanism (carbocation intermediate).`},{title:`Order of reactivity`,diff:`easy`,q:`Arrange CH₃CH₂F, CH₃CH₂Cl, CH₃CH₂Br, CH₃CH₂I in order of increasing reactivity towards NaOH(aq).`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Reactivity is determined by the C–Hal bond strength: weaker bond → faster reaction.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`C–F (484 kJ mol⁻¹) > C–Cl (338) > C–Br (285) > C–I (213).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Weakest bond first → most reactive. Strongest bond last → least reactive.`}],ans:`CH₃CH₂F < CH₃CH₂Cl < CH₃CH₂Br < CH₃CH₂I (least to most reactive).`},{title:`Choose conditions: substitution or elimination`,diff:`medium`,q:`How would you convert 2-bromobutane to (a) butan-2-ol and (b) but-2-ene?`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`For an alcohol product → use SUBSTITUTION conditions: aqueous NaOH or KOH, reflux.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Equation (a): CH₃CHBrCH₂CH₃ + NaOH(aq) → CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ + NaBr.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`For an alkene product → use ELIMINATION conditions: alcoholic KOH, hot, concentrated.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Equation (b): CH₃CHBrCH₂CH₃ + KOH(alc) → CH₃CH=CHCH₃ + KBr + H₂O. (Major product is but-2-ene by Saytzeff.)`}],ans:`(a) aqueous NaOH, reflux → butan-2-ol. (b) alcoholic KOH, hot, conc → but-2-ene.`},{title:`Synthesis with chain extension`,diff:`hard`,q:`Outline how you would convert bromoethane (C₂H₅Br) into propanoic acid (CH₃CH₂COOH).`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Need to extend the chain by 1 carbon. The KCN reaction does this.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Step 1: CH₃CH₂Br + KCN(alc) → CH₃CH₂CN + KBr. Reflux in ethanol. Now have 3 carbons (propanenitrile).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Step 2: hydrolyse the nitrile. CH₃CH₂CN + 2H₂O + HCl → CH₃CH₂COOH + NH₄Cl. Reflux with dilute HCl (or H₂SO₄).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Overall: 2 steps, chain extends C₂ → C₃.`}],ans:`Step 1: bromoethane + KCN(alc) → propanenitrile. Step 2: hydrolyse with HCl → propanoic acid.`},{title:`Identify halogenoalkane`,diff:`medium`,q:`A halogenoalkane X reacts with AgNO₃/ethanol/water to give a yellow precipitate after a short time. Identify the halogen and predict whether the reaction is SN1 or SN2 if X is (CH₃)₃CI.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Yellow precipitate = AgI → halogen is iodine.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Short time = fast hydrolysis. Iodides react fastest because C–I bond is weakest.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`(CH₃)₃CI is a TERTIARY iodoalkane.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Tertiary halogenoalkanes prefer SN1 (carbocation stabilised by 3 alkyl groups).`}],ans:`Halogen = I. (CH₃)₃CI reacts via SN1.`},{title:`Write SN1 mechanism`,diff:`medium`,q:`Show the SN1 mechanism for the hydrolysis of (CH₃)₃CBr.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`STEP 1 (slow, RDS): C–Br bond breaks heterolytically. Both electrons go to Br.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`(CH₃)₃CBr → (CH₃)₃C⁺ + Br⁻. Curly arrow: from C–Br bond to Br.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`STEP 2 (fast): water (or OH⁻) attacks the planar carbocation.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`(CH₃)₃C⁺ + H₂O → (CH₃)₃COH₂⁺ → (CH₃)₃COH (after losing H⁺). Curly arrow: from O lone pair to C⁺.`}],ans:`2-step mechanism: heterolysis to carbocation, then nucleophile attack on planar C⁺.`},{title:`Why excess NH₃?`,diff:`medium`,q:`Explain why excess ammonia is needed when reacting bromoethane with NH₃ to make ethanamine.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`CH₃CH₂Br + NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HBr (primary amine forms).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`BUT the primary amine has a lone pair on N — it is itself a nucleophile.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`It can attack another bromoethane: CH₃CH₂NH₂ + CH₃CH₂Br → (CH₃CH₂)₂NH + HBr (secondary amine).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`This continues: secondary → tertiary → quaternary ammonium salt.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Excess NH₃ ensures statistically that NH₃ is more likely to attack than the amine products → primary amine is the major product.`}],ans:`Without excess NH₃, the primary amine reacts further to give 2°, 3°, and quaternary products.`},{title:`Chlorobenzene reactivity`,diff:`hard`,q:`Explain why chlorobenzene is much less reactive than chloroethane towards aqueous NaOH.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`In chlorobenzene, the lone pair on Cl can overlap with the π electron system of the benzene ring.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`This delocalisation creates partial double-bond character in the C–Cl bond — the bond is shorter and stronger than in chloroethane (399 vs 338 kJ mol⁻¹).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`A stronger bond requires more energy to break → higher activation energy for substitution.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Additionally, the benzene ring is electron-rich (π electrons). It repels approaching nucleophiles like OH⁻, making attack at the C–Cl carbon difficult.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Combined effect: chlorobenzene does not hydrolyse under normal conditions.`}],ans:`Cl lone pair → π overlap → stronger C–Cl bond + electron-rich ring repels OH⁻ → very high Ea.`}],tutorPrompts:[`Walk me through the SN1 mechanism for tert-butyl bromide hydrolysis`,`Why does bromoethane give a primary amine with NH₃ but propanenitrile with KCN?`,`Explain why aqueous KOH gives substitution but alcoholic KOH gives elimination`,`Why is chlorobenzene so much less reactive than chloroethane?`,`Give me a chain-extension synthesis problem using KCN`]};export{e as HALOGEN_DERIV};